            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack") 



** Around the outside of the two doors



** The dryer vent - unfortunately I was not able to check this from the exterior, but I checked the outside vent and noted that it is quite old. I recommend that you investigate this further, and consider replacing the dryer vent. 



** Electrical outlets in the exterior walls of your house showed a significant amount of air leakage. These can be sealed by adding foam gaskets behind the faceplates. 



** There is also significant air leakage arount the pellet stove flue. 



** I did not check all of your new windows, but the frames I did check appeared to be well sealed. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R20 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R20 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $770 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $8,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. My visit on 12 January 2021 satisfies the pre-retrofit requirement. 



The Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program has been extended until March 31 2024, pending available funding, and completion of an exterior insulation retrofit allows to you access additional funding for windows, air sealing, and heating equipment. If you are interested in participating in this program, please contact me for more information. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 2x6 framing and insulation, and your basement pony walls appear to be 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding R20 rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior of the pony walls at the same time as you add insulation to your main floor walls. 



Savings: By adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially save $410 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. Insulation rebates are prorated based on the area that you insulate. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

You have already upgraded most of your windows, but I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading your two front windows and your four basement windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



I have also upgraded the two windows in your doors.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $370 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 11.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <doors>

Both of the exterior doors in this house are older wood doors with single pane glass windows. I recommend you upgrade both of these older doors to an ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone.



Rebates: By replacing these 2 older doors with ones that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified for our climate zone, you could potentially save $140 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 86.4% efficient oil boiler and a pellet boiler. I have modeled an upgrade of your oil boiler to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



PLEASE NOTE: The savings listed in this section assume that you are not using your pellet boiler at all, due to the standard set of assumptions used to rate your house. Please see the end of this section for some notes specific to your pellet boiler. 



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $1,790 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



When assembling the model for determining the energy rating of your home, only heating appliances that use oil, propane, or natural gas are assumed to provide space heating, and any biomass appliances (wood or wood pellets) are modelled but it is assumed that they are not used. However, understanding that you use both your pellet boiler and pellet stove as your primary heat sources and keep your oil boiler as back up, I have given this upgrade a very low priority.



Using your pellet boiler to meet 100% of the heating load on your main floor, you will potentially save $1,000 per year in energy costs. This does not take into account the fact that your pellet boiler is also heating your basement - the software is unable to model this. 



Using your pellet stove to provide 25% of the heating load for your basement, you will potentially save $670 per year in energy costs. 



The savings for your pellet boiler and pellet stove have been calculated separately. and the actual savings you see will vary depending on how often you use each appliance, and how often you rely on the oil boiler for back up. 



To get a rough estimate of your total savings taking both floors into account, I took 75% of your total estimated oil use for both space heating and hot water, and assumed that that oil would be replaced by pellets. Based on the efficiency of your pellet boiler and the total heating load of your house, you will potentially save $2,000 per year in this case.  </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM Pumps

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency. These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



All of your hydronic circulation pumps for your pellet boiler and back-up oil boiler can be replaced with ECM circumlation pumps. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



You have older T4 light fixtures in both your boiler room and your laundry room. While you may not use these rooms regularly, you could consider replacing these fixtures with LEDs. 



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.9 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - three bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer

        - a range hood



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat, which you have already installed.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,

Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
